                    briefing_text_r1:       `The board's verdict is blunt: <em>"our competitive advantage is migrating to technologies we do not own."</em> Of 480 patent families, 71% cluster in electric machines and thermal management — domains whose price-per-unit is falling 6–8% a year. In power semiconductors and embedded software, where margin and differentiation are migrating, you hold <strong>under 9%</strong> of your patents and license core IP. Diagnose the <strong>shape</strong> of your knowledge base — its breadth across domains and depth within each — and name the two gap domains against the value-migration map. The trap: doubling down on the commoditising strongholds, or chasing a fashionable adjacency the flagship customer never asked for.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Build, buy, or partner for each competence`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The core decision. For power semiconductors and embedded software, choose a sourcing mode and fund it from the <strong>€220M</strong> budget — the moves do not all fit. The Eindhoven acquisition is <strong>€140M</strong> (deep, fast, integration-risky); the controls co-development deal is <strong>€35M</strong> (fast, shared IP); the institute joint lab is <strong>€18M</strong> (cheap, broad, slow). Building organically takes 4–5 years — too slow for the 9-month clock. Critically, fund <strong>absorptive capacity</strong>: buying a chip start-up with no internal team to receive the knowledge gives you a black box, not a capability.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose the ecosystem position`,
