                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_growth:        `<strong>You aimed at where value is migrating.</strong> Power semiconductors and embedded software are exactly the domains where margin and differentiation are moving — and where you hold under 9% of your patents. That is the gap that decides the next decade.`,
                    coach_r1_strongholds:   `<strong>You doubled down on the commoditising domains.</strong> Electric machines and thermal management are your strongholds, but their price-per-unit is falling 6–8% a year. Pouring the budget into a domain that is commoditising leaves you deep in yesterday's value. Where is the margin actually moving?`,
                    coach_r1_adjacent:      `<strong>That adjacency is a distraction.</strong> Battery cells and charging are fashionable, but the flagship customer is asking for an integrated power-electronics-plus-software module — not cells. Chasing the wrong gap burns the budget the platform decision needs.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read the shape correctly — narrow but deep, with the dangerous gaps exactly where value is migrating. That is the architecture lens: not how much you spend, but which competences you assemble against where the industry is heading.`,
                    coach_r1_broad:         `<strong>"Broad enough" is how firms get caught.</strong> 480 patent families look impressive, but 71% cluster in two commoditising domains. Breadth that does not cover where value is moving is breadth in the wrong place.`,
                    coach_r1_complacent:    `<strong>The board is not over-reacting.</strong> "Deep everywhere, no real gap" is precisely the complacency that lets value migrate out from under a firm. You are a buyer, not an owner, in the two domains that now matter most.`,
                    coach_r1_lowurgency:    `<strong>You under-weighted the clock.</strong> The sourcing decision is in 9 months and building organically takes 4–5 years. Treating the make-vs-buy-vs-partner choice as non-urgent is how you arrive with a roadmap and no product.`,
                    coach_r2_buyabsorb:     `<strong>You bought depth and funded the team to absorb it.</strong> The Eindhoven acquisition gives you semiconductor capability fast, and the absorptive-capacity investment means your engineers can actually receive and extend it — not inherit a black box.`,
                    coach_r2_blackbox:      `<strong>You bought a logo, not a capability.</strong> You spent €140M on the semiconductor start-up but funded almost no integration team. Eighteen months in, the founders leave and the IP is a black box your engineers cannot extend. Buying knowledge without absorptive capacity destroys value.`,
                    coach_r2_rentsemis:     `<strong>You are renting your future in semiconductors.</strong> The joint lab is cheap, broad, and non-exclusive — capital-efficient, but you own no proprietary IP in a domain where value is migrating, and your partners can sell the same capability to your rivals. Which competence is too strategic to rent?`,
                    coach_r2_buildsemis:    `<strong>Building semiconductors organically misses the clock.</strong> Your own estimate says 4–5 years; the platform sourcing decision is in 9 months. You will arrive with a roadmap and no product, lose €410M in lifetime revenue, and watch a rival who partnered ship. When is building pride, not strategy?`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>Mode matched to the competence.</strong> Buy the deep semiconductor capability you cannot build in time, partner fast for the software where speed matters most, and fund the team to absorb both. That is sourcing matched to the clock and to strategic importance.`,
                    coach_r2_buildsw:       `<strong>Building software organically is too slow.</strong> 4–5 years against a 9-month clock. Software-controls is exactly where a fast co-development deal beats an organic programme that delivers long after the platform is awarded.`,
                    coach_r2_buysw:         `<strong>A second acquisition breaks the budget.</strong> There is no funded software target, and stacking another buy on top of the €140M Eindhoven deal blows through your €220M cap. Capability investment needs critical mass within the constraint, not over it.`,
                    coach_r2_noabsorb:      `<strong>You funded the moves but not the absorption.</strong> Without internal teams to receive the knowledge, even a good acquisition or partnership decays into something you own on paper but cannot extend. Absorptive capacity is the difference between owning and capability.`,
                    coach_r2_overstronghold: `<strong>You over-funded a commoditising stronghold.</strong> A little defence of electric machines and thermal is rational, but pouring budget into a domain whose prices are falling 6–8% a year starves the gap domains where the platform is actually won.`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>You spread the €220M too thin.</strong> Partnering for both competences with little absorptive capacity under-funds every move below critical mass — the build is too small to reach depth and no competence actually closes. What is the focused bet here?`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid posture fits a firm with crown jewels and gaps.</strong> Closed where you have defensible IP, open as an orchestrator where you need external breadth — that is how you draw on the Grenoble cluster without leaking your stronghold.`,
                    coach_r3_closed:        `<strong>Closed everywhere starves you of breadth.</strong> A pure vertical integrator controls its IP but cuts itself off from the institute, the universities and the start-ups two desks away. You cannot absorb knowledge you have walled yourself off from.`,
                    coach_r3_open:          `<strong>Maximum openness buys breadth — and risk.</strong> Opening the whole portfolio gives you real absorptive gains, but openness is a strategy, not a virtue. Which domains are too valuable to open? Watch the IP boundary in the next decision.`,
                    coach_r3_boundary_right: `<strong>That is the open/closed boundary done on purpose.</strong> Crown-jewel thermal management stays closed; the new growth domains open to partners so you can access breadth fast without leaking your last defensible IP. Openness as a deliberate design.`,
                    coach_r3_leak:          `<strong>You opened your last defensible stronghold.</strong> Putting thermal-management IP into a joint lab that includes a firm two desks from your competitor leaks the crown jewels. "Open everywhere" is as naive as "closed everywhere" — which domains had to stay closed?`,
                    coach_r3_closeall:      `<strong>Closing the partnered domains defeats the partnership.</strong> If you wall off even the domains you chose to access through the cluster, you cannot absorb what the partners bring. The boundary should protect the jewels, not strangle the openness you deliberately chose.`,
                    coach_r3_nointernalise: `<strong>Owning is not absorbing.</strong> With low effort to internalise, external knowledge sits on a shelf — patents and licences you hold but cannot convert into firm-specific capability. Absorptive capacity is deliberate work, not a line item.`,
                    coach_r4_module:        `<strong>Output that reached the customer.</strong> Leading with the integrated module shipping on time and winning the platform is the right scorecard — innovation output that serves the one sourcing decision that mattered, not patents nobody asked for.`,
                    coach_r4_patents:       `<strong>Patents filed is not the same as a product shipped.</strong> Sixty new patent families look impressive on paper, but if the integrated module is not ready you lose the platform — and most of those patents are in domains the customer was not asking for. That is R&D theatre.`,
                    coach_r4_activity:      `<strong>Signed deals are inputs, not output.</strong> Three partnerships and a closed acquisition are activity; the board is asking what the knowledge base produced. Activity that does not converge on the module is motion, not progress.`,
                    coach_r4_agile:         `<strong>You shipped AND stayed positioned.</strong> Winning this platform while keeping staged real-option bets in adjacent domains means that when value migrates again in three years, you are positioned to respond — not repeating this crisis.`,
                    coach_r4_lockin:        `<strong>Locking in leaves you exposed to the next migration.</strong> Pouring everything into the one domain you just bought, with no optionality, is exactly how Aurelis got narrow the first time. Value will migrate again — keep some options open.`,
                    coach_r4_react:         `<strong>Waiting to react repeats the crisis.</strong> Only investing once the next migration is obvious is how you ended up a buyer, not an owner, in semiconductors and software. Reshaping ahead of migration is the discipline the board demanded.`,
                    coach_r4_theatre:       `<strong>Defending with patents the customer never asked for is theatre.</strong> Filings in the wrong domains do not win the platform. The board can see the difference between innovation output that reaches the customer and activity dressed up as strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_spend:         `<strong>Spending the full budget is not a result.</strong> "We committed all €220M, so it must be working" confuses input with output. Investment efficiency is output produced per euro — not euros committed.`,
                    coach_overbudget:       `<strong>You breached the €220M cap.</strong> The board approved a hard constraint, and over-committing across moves means none of them reaches critical mass cleanly — the over-spend itself signals a strategy that did not fit the budget. Re-balance within the cap.`,

